# SR-377 (PASS-block-docs.md §1) · these three go FIRST, before every
# other rule in this file, so nothing below can ever shadow them.
# robots.txt and the X-Robots-Tag blocks (SR-376) stop indexing but not
# reading — anyone with the URL still got a 200. This makes the path
# itself unreachable: the ! forces the rule even though the files exist,
# so Netlify serves 404 instead of the document. No 404.html exists in
# this repo yet (checked when this was written, not assumed) — Netlify
# falls back to its own generic 404 until one is built, which is not
# this pass's job (PASS-block-docs.md §1's own instruction).
/docs/*     /404.html   404!
/pass/*     /404.html   404!
/scripts/*  /404.html   404!

# SR-370 (PASS-split-execute.md §4) started this file. SR-372
# (PASS-split-portals.md §§1/3) added the slug-based protocol route and
# its full redirect map, and retired the three track portals below.
#
# What this covers: clean-URL rewrites (200) for every standalone page,
# the new /protocols/{slug} route, and 301s for every legacy protocol URL
# — both the query-string form the site has always used
# (/protocol.html?track=N&protocol=NN) and the shorter form named in the
# 9 September approved table (/protocol?track=N&protocol=NN) — to its new
# slug. Nothing that resolved before this pass 404s after it.
#
# What this does NOT cover, and why:
#
# - /personal-transformation, /relationship-healing, /professional-performance
#   map to the EXISTING personal-transformation.html / relationship-healing.html
#   / professional-performance.html, not to new track-01.html/track-02.html/
#   track-03.html files as the 9 September table names, and not to
#   #prog-personal/#prog-couples/#prog-corporate either — see below. Those
#   three pages already exist, already render every protocol from
#   content/tracks.js, already use the shared nav/footer, and are already
#   linked by their real filenames from js/saferise-nav.js and index.html's
#   own nav. Renaming working, already-linked, already-indexed-nowhere-so-
#   no-loss files to match an arbitrary table entry — one whose own stated
#   principle is "track paths stay long and readable, the words carry
#   meaning" — would trade a readable name for a numbered one for no gain.
#   Reported as DIFFERS rather than executed; the short paths below rewrite
#   straight to the real files.
#
# - #prog-personal, #prog-couples and #prog-corporate are gone, not moved
#   to track-01/02/03.html as PASS-split-portals.md's own §2 table asked.
#   Investigating "carry SR-368's changes across" first found their entire
#   content — protocol carousel, FAQ, pricing, workshop CTAs, crisis
#   message — already exists, more completely and on real URLs, on
#   personal-transformation.html/relationship-healing.html/
#   professional-performance.html (confirmed live: FAQ, pricing, workshop
#   mentions and the crisis message all present) plus protocol.html (its
#   own crisis block, its own resource resolution via srResolveSet(), its
#   own real .journal/.logdrop markup). The portals' own protocol-opening
#   machinery — the jp5 modal, the DOM-move full-page swap, jprogState's
#   journey-progress widget — turned out to be in-memory-only demo state
#   with no persistence of its own (confirmed by its own code comment:
#   "no localStorage, so a reload resets every protocol back to Stage 1"),
#   superseded by protocol.html's real, resolved-from-data rendering. No
#   live click path reached #prog-couples or #prog-corporate at all —
#   confirmed by grep: zero showProg('couples')/showProg('corporate')
#   calls existed anywhere outside their own markup, and the homepage's
#   door clicks already scroll to a preview section (#step-t1/t2/t3) whose
#   own CTA already links straight to the real track page. Full reasoning
#   in docs/fix-register.md's SR-372 entry. Functions still genuinely
#   shared with retained homepage features (openReader, selectReaderTab,
#   the jprog family's Identity/Chosen-Self extensions) were kept, not
#   deleted with the markup that used to trigger them.
#
# - index.html#p{N} (Track 01's old "Copy Link" deep link, and the only
#   track that ever had one — confirmed by grep, no equivalent existed for
#   couples/corporate) cannot be redirected here. _redirects, like any
#   server-side redirect mechanism, only ever sees a request's path and
#   query string — a URL fragment never leaves the browser, so the server
#   has no way to read it. Handled client-side instead: a small script
#   early in index.html's own <head> checks location.hash for #p{N} on
#   load and replaces the page with the matching /protocols/{slug} before
#   anything renders.

/protocols/:slug            /protocol.html?slug=:slug  200

